    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-3560995">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-3560995">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,3560995)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-3560995">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/chubert" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(502908); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=502908&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608045302" alt="Chubert" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=502908)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/chubert" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(502908); return false;" >Chubert</a></span> <span class="odate time_1501400203 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">30 Jul 2017 07:36</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3560995">
									

<p>I like the changes you've made. In terms of pacing, starting out with the checkpoint lets you put in world-building and setting description at a more sedate pace before jumping into the more chaotic transit passage. I'm also a much bigger fan of the more extended description of Connie and Amitha's relationship.</p>
<p>While I'm overall enjoying the series, I see two main areas for improvement, one of which is more nitpicky but I can identify more clearly, the other of which affects my reading experience more but I can't identify as clearly.</p>
<p>For the first one, I think you oversell characterization. For example, when Connie and Amitha meet:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Were it not for the assemblage of gears and mechanisms attached to her back and the newly-carved scars under my eyes and on my arms, we would've been the picture-perfect copy of the couple who'd snuck kisses and forbidden literature to one another in that same laboratory a decade or so ago.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Great. A vivid image of their relationship which also implies loss. But you also have, a few paragraphs above, &quot;an almost painful reminder of everything I'd lost with her absence,&quot; which is far weaker prose that you can basically just cut because you do a better job later.</p>
<p>Similarly, in</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Taking a deep breath to still myself, I reached my hand up robotically and rapped on the door with three measured knocks.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You have three things telling me that Connie feels nervous and is trying to calm herself down and you don't need all of them. &quot;Robotically&quot; is, in my view, the weakest link.</p>
<p>As for the thing I can't articulate as well, I think a problem I'm experiencing is that this is super dense in terms of world building, and I'm not sure what I'm missing because it's internal to the tale and you haven't explained it yet and I need to wait, what I'm missing because it's internal to the tale and you already explained it and I just missed it, and what I'm missing because it's external site lore that I'm not super familiar with. In particular, the way &quot;Flipside&quot; interacts with Alagadda (like, what jurisdiction does a Flipside inquisitor have in Alagadda exactly?) kind of eludes me. I'm not sure that there's a clear-cut way to address this, mostly because it's probably something that's more a Chubert thing than a general reader thing.</p>
<p>I'm excited to see how the Black Queen interacts with Connie and Amitha. Also I'm hype for the Offspring chapter name.</p>

							</div>

								
					
